specHash: the 114 byte body from my 17 Oct comment (https://github.com/x402-foundation/x402/issues/2887#issuecomment-6237860774), with POST /api/paid/verify-ownership and a newline in front, gives sha256:54d241a409066f49923f687c46a164f57e1731ec4c825762e451031037aaa359, equal to yours.

outputHash: posting the same body to my free route /api/verify-ownership on api.tokenizen.nl returns 230 bytes whose sha256 is 6eb0d9ff9921e5603fedd64a30c45134408f445450324ea254552d8061d88025

@zambodotdev agreed on the boundary. The step after the receipt is the judgment itself: a party with no stake in the job checks the delivered bytes against criteria committed before payment, and signs the result per criterion.

That is what the acceptance record does (`trilatic.acceptance-receipt/1.2`). Each criterion is `pass`, `fail`, `not-evaluated` or `error`. If a required criterion is `not-evaluated` or `error`, the verdict is `incomplete`, never `pass`, so the settled-but-unanswered case has its own named state there too.

It already runs downstream of settlement in this thread. Job 29
